Just to follow up with a comparison with [Newtonsoft JSON.Net](https://www.newtonsoft.com/) JSONPath, which is another of the dozen implementations in the JSONPath Comparisons that evaluate `@.foo` as a test for existence. Of these dozen implementations, Jayway and Newtonsoft are clearly the most important. Java Jayway is likely the most widely used of all implementations, and is incorporated in Oracle enterprise API's. Newtonsoft JSON.Net shows 2 billion downloads on NuGet.

Given
```json 
[
  [ {"normal": 3711, "fallback": 815},
    {"normal": 4711, "fallback": 10},
    {"normal": 5711, "fallback": 615}
  ],
  [ {"normal": 3711, "fallback": 815},
    {"normal": 4711},
    {"normal": 5711, "fallback": 615}
  ],
  [ {"normal": 3711},
    {"normal": 4711},
    {"normal": 5711}
  ]
]
```
and query
```
$[?(@.*.fallback)]
```
Newtonsoft JSONPath returns no results. However, given, to my eye, the more natural query

```
$[?(@[?(@.fallback)])]
```
Newtonsoft JSONPath returns 
```json
[
  [
    {    "normal": 3711,    "fallback": 815  },
    {    "normal": 4711,    "fallback": 10  },
    {    "normal": 5711,    "fallback": 615  }
  ],
  [
    {    "normal": 3711,    "fallback": 815  },
    {    "normal": 4711  },
    {    "normal": 5711,    "fallback": 615  }
  ]
]
```

Jayway, in contrast, when given
```
$[?(@[?(@.fallback)])]
```
returns everything,

```json 
[
  [ {"normal": 3711, "fallback": 815},
    {"normal": 4711, "fallback": 10},
    {"normal": 5711, "fallback": 615}
  ],
  [ {"normal": 3711, "fallback": 815},
    {"normal": 4711},
    {"normal": 5711, "fallback": 615}
